rrative stakes will also see  major characters take riskier actions  with greater chances of having them  being lost permanently depending on the  choices you make although the inherent  satisfaction of the game's combat system  can exist without context nearly every  other aspect of the Banner Saga 3 relies  on having some prior knowledge for  meaningful enjoyment for example combat  units who enjoyed significant character  arcs in banner saga and banner saga  2 may only have a small handful of light  in three which can give the wrong  impression of flimsy characterization  starting a new game from within three  will let you make a major binary  decision that arose  the end of Banner Saga one but the  multitude of other choices and potential  consequences from the first 20 or so  hours of the series are made for you and  the game doesn't spend time reading the  complex relationship between characters  unique races and the world itself but if  you have played through the first two  games and ideally imported a save file  through them both then you'll have a  much better idea of how catastrophic  things are going into Banner Saga 3 in  this situation the final game does a  wonderful job of taking the discrete set  of experiences you've had with your  roster of aspiring characters as well as  your long-term resources that you've  acquired and using them as emotional  leverage creating meaningful personal  impacts as the story climaxes and  concludes I've had a bad feeling that  these things will never again return to  the way they once were  even if we succeed desperation also  informs additions to the turn and grid  based combat system the signature  mechanics remain strength is a valley  that informs both your health and attack  power meaning the less healthy have the  weak year attack an armor value protects  against strength damage and units can  choose to focus an attack on either  strength or armor world power returns as  a limited resource that feels unique  character abilities as well as allowing  you to overexert in basic actions to  either move further or hit harder but a  significant number of scenarios are now  wave based helping to characterize the  relentless tides of enemies trying to  lay siege to our brain and stop the  mercenaries at all costs failing to stop  a wave in a set amount of time results  in enemy lineups stacking together  resulting in an overwhelming number of  adversaries and all but certain failure  successfully defeating a wave gives you  the choice of continuing to fight or  fleeing the battle as well as an  opportunity to substitute the characters  in your active force completing an  entire battle will reward you with a  high-end item to equip the higher  narrative stakes and the stronger  enemy's means that you need to fight  diligently the circumstances mean there  aren't many opportunities to give your  characters time to recover from  debilitating injuries after battle if  they fall and even when you do get a  chance to make them rest it's at the  cost of precious time before the  Doomsday Clock runs out  failing or fleeing a battle after a wave  or two can also have negative  consequences in the plot the  substitution system is engaging because  it not only asks you to draw from the  sizable roster of characters you may or  may not have collected and kept alive of  the past two games but it  man's that you keep them all upgraded  equipped and battle ready rather than  letting you rely on a small stable  roster the Banner Saga 3 calls for all  hands on deck and motivates to make the  most of what you've gathered in the past  to stay prepared for the worst the worst  will come in the form of new warped  enemies mutated and more powerful  versions of the series existing units  whose behaviors add new emphasis on  spatial considerations all warped enemy  variants explode upon dying creating  hazardous squares and require more  careful movement around the battlefield  other environmental elements like  spreading fires and exploding ice shards  feel like bigger considerations to sum  around and are more inviting to take  advantage of strategically given the  more numerous opposition one significant  Haulover annoyance remains from the  previous games however the fixed  isometric perspective of combat means  units can often obscure the contents of  the grid tiles behind them and while  you're given the opportunity to utilize  a handful of new and exciting units  yourself depending on your choices  there's a lot that's become absent since  the Banner Saga - even though they might  fit the narrative combat training and  challenge missions have been lost as  have a lot of the series as cloud  management interactions while you'll  still need to keep an eye on each  group's overall morale supplies and  population there are very few  opportunities to bolster these resources  the Banner Saga is no longer a game that  revolves around traveling and recruiting  and growing but one that's a constant  task of trying to stave off attrition as  best you can  during the last end as things come down  to the wire with the two separate groups  becoming more and more interconnected  the consequences of one group's actions  directly affect the potential of the  other this exemplifies the best part of  Banner Saga 3 the feeling that every  action you've made in regards to your  clan for three games the friends you've  kept and lost the decisions you've made  and the battles you've picked will  likely influence how well this finale is  gonna fare for you the Banner Saga 3  won't have the same kind of meaningful  impact if you haven't experienced the  rest of the series but if you've taken  the time to journey with these  characters from the very beginning this  finale is a worthwhile a cathartic end  to your long journey all I care about  now is that the friends  no the family I fought to deliver to our  brown left to see the Sun move again
 

